window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("llmops-platform", {"topics":[{"key":"llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","sources":["Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness and Technical Debt Reduction","Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems"],"why":"A conventional pipeline rests on three assumptions: the behaviour of the system is determined by the\ncode, tests are deterministic, and the bits that passed are the bits that run in production. An LLM\napplication violates all three. Behaviour is a function of code, prompt text, the model behind an API\nalias, decoding parameters and the retrieval index, and outputs are sampled rather than computed. The\nmodel can also change underneath you: a provider updates the model behind `-latest`, a training job\nproduces a new adapter, an index is re-embedded.\n\nTeams usually try one of three shortcuts first. Keeping prompts as string constants in the codebase\nputs them under review but gives no evidence: a PR that fixes the one complaint in the ticket regresses\nten other cases, and the regression is discovered by users. Letting people edit prompts live in a\ndashboard makes iteration fast but leaves no review, no rollback, and no record of which prompt produced\nwhich answer. Running the entire eval suite on every commit is the correct instinct done badly: thousands\nof model calls per push cost real money and take tens of minutes, so within a month the step is marked\noptional and skipped.\n\nThe practice that works treats prompts and models as first-class release artifacts: the pipeline knows\nwhich artifact changed, runs an eval of depth proportional to the risk of that stage, gates on aggregate\nquality rather than exact strings, and promotes the exact bundle it tested.","how":"### Three artifacts, three cadences\nApplication code changes daily and is covered by deterministic tests. Prompts and configuration change\nhourly to daily, are cheap to evaluate, and are often edited by product managers or forward-deployed\nengineers. Models change weekly to quarterly, are expensive to evaluate, and have capacity and cost\nconsequences. A release is therefore defined as a *bundle*: `{code sha, prompt versions, model id or\nweights revision, decoding params, index snapshot}`. The pipeline tests and promotes bundles, because\ntesting a prompt against one model and deploying it with another tests nothing.\n\n### The test ladder\nDepth grows with the blast radius of the stage, because cost and wall-clock time scale with model calls:\n\n1. **Static and unit tests (seconds, no model calls).** Every template renders with every declared\n   variable; output parsers run against recorded model outputs; tool JSON schemas validate; guardrail\n   rules compile; the rendered prompt stays under its token budget.\n2. **Contract tests with recorded responses.** Replay fixtures through the real code path to check\n   plumbing (retries, streaming, parsing). They prove the wiring, not the quality.\n3. **Smoke eval on every PR (minutes, a few dollars).** A stratified subset of the golden set, 50 to\n   200 items as an illustrative size, scored with deterministic checks where possible and an LLM judge\n   where necessary, compared against the baseline score recorded by the last green build of main.\n4. **Full eval on merge, nightly and on release candidates.** The whole golden set plus slices, cost\n   and latency, with every output stored as a build artifact so reviewers can diff behaviour.\n5. **Deploy with a canary** and online metrics, and an automatic rollback trigger.\n\n### Gating on distributions, not strings\nBecause outputs are sampled, exact-match assertions on free text flake. The pipeline compares score\ndistributions: a mean with a confidence interval against the champion, absolute floors for safety and\nschema validity, and per-slice floors. The stored outputs are the review artifact; the diff between the\ncandidate's and the champion's answers on the same items is what a reviewer actually reads. The\n`evaluation-gates-for-production-releases` lesson covers the statistics.\n\n### Content-addressed caching\nEval cost is dominated by model calls, so every call is keyed by\n`hash(model id, params, prompt version, input)` and cached. A PR that touches one prompt re-runs only the\nitems that use that prompt; a code-only PR often re-runs nothing and just re-scores. This is what makes a\nper-PR eval affordable, and it is also why the cache key must include every pin: a key that omits the\nmodel id will happily serve stale outputs after a model upgrade and hide the change.\n\n### Promote, do not rebuild\nThe bundle that passed the release eval is promoted dev → staging → production by moving a pointer in\nthe prompt and model registries, never by rebuilding at deploy time, because a rebuild re-resolves\nfloating aliases (`-latest`, `main`) and can ship something that was never tested.\n\n### Prompts as code versus prompts as config\nWith prompts in the repository, prompt changes ride the normal PR pipeline; simple, reviewable, but\nnon-engineers are blocked and prompt deploys are coupled to code deploys. With a prompt registry, the\nregistry is the source of truth, a label change is the deploy, and the pipeline is triggered by a\nregistry webhook to run the same eval gate before the `production` label moves. Most mature teams land\non the hybrid: registry as source of truth, pipeline as the only thing allowed to move production labels.\n\n### Model changes are releases too\nPin the model snapshot in the bundle. Run a scheduled \"model upgrade\" job that opens a PR with the new\nsnapshot and the full eval when the provider announces a new version or a deprecation; treat a retrained\nadapter arriving in the model registry the same way. The trigger differs; the ladder is identical.","example":"A PR-time smoke eval that sits as one job in the pipeline. The YAML fragment shows where it runs; the\nscript shows the content-addressed cache and the comparison against the baseline written by the last\ngreen build of `main`. `call_app` and `score` are your harness's entrypoint and scorer.\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/llm-ci.yml (fragment): trigger on anything that changes the bundle\non:\n  pull_request:\n    paths: [\"app/**\", \"prompts/**\", \"release.json\"]\njobs:\n  smoke-eval:\n    steps:\n      - run: python scripts/smoke_eval.py --subset smoke   # nightly job runs --subset full\n```\n\n```python\n# scripts/smoke_eval.py\nimport hashlib, json, pathlib, sys\n\nbundle = json.load(open(\"release.json\"))   # {\"model\": \"vendor-model-2025-04-14\", \"prompt\": \"support_answer@v12\", \"params\": {...}}\nsubset = sys.argv[sys.argv.index(\"--subset\") + 1]\nitems = [json.loads(l) for l in open(f\"data/golden/{subset}.jsonl\")]\ncache = pathlib.Path(\".evalcache\"); cache.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n\ndef key(item):   # every pin is in the key, so an unchanged (model, prompt, params, input) never re-calls the model\n    blob = json.dumps([bundle[\"model\"], bundle[\"prompt\"], bundle[\"params\"], item[\"input\"]], sort_keys=True)\n    return hashlib.sha256(blob.encode()).hexdigest()\n\ndef run_item(item):\n    p = cache / key(item)\n    if p.exists():\n        return json.loads(p.read_text())\n    out = call_app(bundle, item[\"input\"])                 # the production code path, not a copy of it\n    rec = {\"id\": item[\"id\"], \"output\": out, \"score\": score(item, out)}\n    p.write_text(json.dumps(rec)); return rec\n\nresults = [run_item(i) for i in items]\nmean = sum(r[\"score\"] for r in results) / len(results)\nbaseline = json.load(open(\"eval_baselines/main.json\"))[\"mean\"]   # written by the last green build of main\njson.dump({\"bundle\": bundle, \"mean\": mean, \"items\": results}, open(\"eval_out.json\", \"w\"))  # stored as a build artifact\nprint(f\"candidate={mean:.3f} baseline={baseline:.3f} n={len(results)}\")\nsys.exit(0 if mean >= baseline - 0.02 else 1)   # smoke tolerance; the release gate uses a paired CI instead\n```","tradeoffs":["Per-PR smoke evals catch gross regressions in minutes, but a 100-item subset cannot resolve a one- or two-point change; the moment you need that resolution you must pay for the full set or a canary.","Caching makes evals cheap and fast, yet every omitted pin in the cache key becomes a way to hide a real change; the more aggressive the cache, the more disciplined the key must be.","Prompts-as-config decouples prompt deploys from code deploys and unblocks non-engineers, at the cost of a second deploy path that must be gated as strictly as the first or it becomes the bypass everyone uses.","Gating on aggregate scores tolerates sampling noise, but it also tolerates an individual catastrophic answer; safety and schema checks therefore need absolute per-item floors, not averages.","Pinning model snapshots gives reproducible builds, but snapshots get deprecated; without a scheduled upgrade job, pinning turns into an emergency migration on the provider's timetable."],"pitfalls":["Running evals against a copy of the prompt chain inside the test harness instead of the production entrypoint; the symptom is a green pipeline and a production regression caused by code the eval never executed.","Comparing the candidate against a fixed threshold rather than the current champion; quality erodes a point per release while every release passes, and nobody notices until a customer does.","Retrying a flaky eval until it goes green; the symptom is a gate whose pass rate is uncorrelated with quality and a team that has stopped reading the numbers.","Letting the `production` label in the registry be moved from the UI without the pipeline; the symptom is an incident whose timeline contains a prompt change with no PR, no eval and no author.","Using `-latest` model aliases in the bundle; the symptom is a score change on a day with no commits, traced eventually to a provider update."],"when":"Introduce this the moment more than one person edits prompts or a prompt is deployed to real users:\nthat is when \"we changed something and it got worse\" becomes unanswerable. Start with the cheapest\nrungs, unit tests on templates and parsers plus a 50-item smoke eval against a stored baseline, and add\nthe full suite and canary when release frequency or user impact justifies them. Measure pipeline cost\nper PR, eval wall-clock time, cache hit rate and, most importantly, the number of production regressions\nthat the pipeline would have caught. In interviews the expected answer to \"how do you deploy a prompt\nchange safely\" is this ladder: unit, recorded-response contract, smoke eval versus baseline, full eval\nwith gates, canary with rollback, with the bundle, not the prompt, as the unit of release.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the smoke-eval job, the content-addressed cache and the baseline file, and keeps the harness calling the real application entrypoint rather than a reimplementation.","architect":"Decides that the bundle is the unit of release, which registry owns each artifact, and that the pipeline is the only path allowed to move a production label.","fde":"Reproduces a lightweight version of the ladder inside the customer's CI, often with the customer's own golden set, so their change-management process has evidence rather than a demo."},"check":{"q":"A product manager edits a system prompt in the registry UI at 4 pm and moves the `production`\nlabel; latency is unchanged but support tickets about wrong refund amounts double overnight. The code\npipeline is green. What went wrong structurally, and how would you change the pipeline so it cannot\nhappen again?","a":"Structurally, the prompt had a deploy path that bypassed every test: the code pipeline only runs on\ncommits, and the registry label move was an unreviewed, un-evaluated production change. The fix is not\nto forbid non-engineers from editing prompts but to make the label move itself the thing the pipeline\ngates. Registry edits create a new immutable version; a webhook or scheduled job runs the smoke eval and,\nfor `production`, the full eval and gates against the champion on the same golden set, including a\nrefund-intent slice with a floor; only the pipeline's service account may move the production label,\nand it writes the eval evidence next to the version. Rollback is moving the label back. Finally, add the\nincident's cases to the golden set so the regression is a permanent test."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the PR stage run a stratified subset of the golden set rather than the full suite?","options":["Subsets are statistically more reliable than the full set","Eval cost and time scale with model calls, so depth is matched to the blast radius of each stage","Full suites can only be run against production models","GitHub Actions cannot run jobs longer than ten minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every item is a model call that costs money and time; a per-PR job must stay fast and cheap or it will be skipped. The full suite runs on merge, nightly and on release candidates where the stakes justify it."},{"q":"Why should the tested bundle be promoted by pointer rather than rebuilt at deploy time?","options":["Rebuilding is slower because weights must be downloaded again","Registries cannot store more than one version at a time","A rebuild re-resolves floating aliases and can deploy a model or prompt that was never evaluated","Promotion avoids the need for a canary stage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The whole point of the gate is that the exact bits that passed are the bits that run. A rebuild that resolves `-latest` or `main` again may pick up a provider update or a prompt edit the eval never saw."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to put the model snapshot id inside the eval cache key?","options":["It makes the cache directory easier to browse","Providers require it for billing attribution","Without it, a model upgrade would be served stale cached outputs and the change would be invisible","Hashing is cheaper when the key is longer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cache exists to skip calls whose inputs have not changed. If the model id is not part of the input definition, swapping the model counts as \"unchanged\" and the eval silently measures the old model."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/prompt-registry","sources":["Official docs: Langfuse","Official docs: MLflow"],"why":"Prompts start life as string constants. That is fine for one engineer and one prompt. It stops being\nfine the first time a product manager wants to change the tone of the answer, a forward-deployed\nengineer needs a customer-specific variant, or an incident review asks \"which prompt produced this\nresponse at 02:14 on Tuesday\". With constants, every tweak is a code deploy, variants become nested\nif-statements, and the trace holds the rendered text at best and nothing at worst.\n\nThe naive upgrade, a `prompts` table edited from an admin page, solves the deploy problem and creates a\nworse one: a mutable row has no history, so a bad edit is indistinguishable from the previous good one,\nthere is no review step, no eval evidence attached to a change, and rollback means remembering what the\ntext used to be. Teams that go this route tend to discover the problem at 3 am.\n\nThe registry pattern separates the two things people conflate: the *content* of a prompt, which must be\nimmutable once created so that every past trace can be resolved forever, and the *pointer* that says\nwhich content a given environment uses, which must be mutable so that deploy and rollback are a pointer\nmove. It is the same split a model registry makes between versions and aliases, applied to text.","how":"### Data model\nA **prompt** is a named entity (`support_answer`). Each **version** is immutable and holds the template\nbody, usually a list of chat messages with roles rather than a single string, the declared variables and\ntheir types, the model settings it was tuned against (model id, temperature, max tokens, output schema),\na content digest, the author, a change note and a created timestamp. Versions are append-only because\na trace that recorded `support_answer@v12` must resolve to the same text in a year.\n\nA **label** is a mutable pointer from `(prompt, label)` to a version: `production`, `staging`, `canary`,\nor tenant-scoped labels such as `tenant:acme`. Deploy is a label move; rollback is the same operation in\nreverse; an A/B test is two labels resolved by a routing rule. Labels are mutable because the\nalternative, deploying code to change which version runs, is exactly what the registry exists to avoid.\n\nAttached to a version, not a label, are the **evidence** records: eval run fingerprints and scores on a\nnamed golden-set version, and links to the review that approved it. Evidence belongs to content, not to\npointers, because the question \"was this text tested\" has one answer regardless of where it is deployed.\n\n### Prompt and model are a pair\nA prompt is tuned to a model. The same text that works on one model family may over-refuse, ignore the\nschema or change tone on another, so a version stores model id and decoding parameters alongside the\ntext. Swapping the model under an unchanged prompt is a new bundle and must be re-evaluated; the registry\nmakes that visible by refusing to pretend the text alone is the artifact.\n\n### Runtime fetch\nThe application fetches by name and label at request time through an SDK that keeps an in-memory copy\nwith a short TTL, refreshes in the background, and serves the stale copy if the registry is unreachable.\nA bundled copy of the production versions ships with the application as a last-resort fallback. The\nregistry is therefore never on the hot path in the sense that its outage cannot take the product down,\nwhich is the condition under which it is acceptable to let it control behaviour at all.\n\nEvery model call logs the prompt name, version and digest into the trace. Observability platforms that\ninclude prompt management, Langfuse being the common open-source example, link a trace to the prompt\nversion automatically, which turns \"find every answer produced by v12\" into a filter.\n\n### Change workflow\nCreate a draft version, run the eval suite against it (the registry or the CI pipeline triggers this),\nattach the results, get a review, move the label. The pipeline's service account should be the only\nprincipal allowed to move `production`, so that an edit in the UI can create a version but cannot\ndeploy it. A diff view between two versions, both text and settings, is the review artifact.\n\n### Where to host it\nPrompts in a git directory with a build step give review and history for free but couple prompt\ndeploys to code deploys unless the application loads them from an object store at runtime. Hosted\nregistries in observability or eval platforms (Langfuse, LangSmith, Braintrust; MLflow added a prompt\nregistry in its 3.x line) give labels, a UI for non-engineers and trace linkage. A home-grown table with\nversions and labels is a few hundred lines and is often the right answer for a small platform team that\nalready owns a config service. What matters is the split between immutable versions and mutable labels,\nnot the vendor.","example":"A minimal registry showing the three operations that matter: register an immutable version (identical\ncontent maps to the existing version, so re-registering is idempotent), move a label, and fetch with a\nTTL cache that serves stale on failure. A real deployment backs `store` with a database.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time\n\nclass PromptRegistry:\n    def __init__(self, store):                 # store: {\"versions\": {name: [...]}, \"labels\": {(name, label): {...}}}\n        self.store, self._cache = store, {}\n\n    def register(self, name, messages, model, params, author, note):\n        body = json.dumps({\"messages\": messages, \"model\": model, \"params\": params}, sort_keys=True)\n        digest = hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n        versions = self.store[\"versions\"].setdefault(name, [])\n        for v in versions:\n            if v[\"digest\"] == digest:\n                return v[\"version\"]            # same content, same version: registering is idempotent\n        v = {\"version\": len(versions) + 1, \"digest\": digest, \"messages\": messages, \"model\": model,\n             \"params\": params, \"author\": author, \"note\": note, \"created\": time.time(), \"evals\": {}}\n        versions.append(v)                     # append-only: a version is never edited or deleted\n        return v[\"version\"]\n\n    def set_label(self, name, label, version, by):      # deploy and rollback are both this one call\n        self.store[\"labels\"][(name, label)] = {\"version\": version, \"by\": by, \"at\": time.time()}\n\n    def get(self, name, label=\"production\", ttl=60):\n        now, hit = time.time(), self._cache.get((name, label))\n        if hit and now - hit[\"at\"] < ttl:\n            return hit[\"v\"]\n        try:\n            ver = self.store[\"labels\"][(name, label)][\"version\"]\n            v = self.store[\"versions\"][name][ver - 1]\n            self._cache[(name, label)] = {\"v\": v, \"at\": now}\n            return v\n        except Exception:\n            if hit:\n                return hit[\"v\"]                # registry down: serve stale rather than fail the request\n            raise\n\nreg = PromptRegistry({\"versions\": {}, \"labels\": {}})\nv1 = reg.register(\"support_answer\",\n                  [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Answer using only the provided context: {{context}}\"},\n                   {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"{{question}}\"}],\n                  model=\"vendor-model-2025-04-14\",        # a dated snapshot id, never a floating alias\n                  params={\"temperature\": 0.2, \"max_tokens\": 600}, author=\"ana\", note=\"initial\")\nreg.set_label(\"support_answer\", \"production\", v1, by=\"ci:release-42\")\np = reg.get(\"support_answer\")\n# Log p[\"version\"] and p[\"digest\"] on every trace so any answer resolves to its exact prompt.\n```","tradeoffs":["Decoupling prompt deploys from code deploys lets non-engineers iterate in minutes, but it creates a second production change path that must be gated as strictly as code or it becomes the bypass.","Immutable versions guarantee every trace resolves forever, at the cost of a growing store and a retention policy; for most teams prompts are kilobytes and this never matters, for multi-tenant platforms with thousands of variants it does.","Runtime fetching keeps behaviour changeable without redeploy, but it adds a dependency; the TTL cache and bundled fallback remove the availability risk while introducing a propagation delay of one TTL.","Storing model settings with the prompt makes the pair explicit and re-evaluation mandatory on model change; it also means a platform-wide model migration touches every prompt version rather than one config line.","A hosted registry gives UI, labels and trace linkage out of the box; a git directory gives review and blame for free. The hosted option wins once people outside engineering own prompts."],"pitfalls":["Editing a version in place \"because it is just a typo\"; the symptom is two traces with the same version id and different rendered text, which destroys every post-incident analysis that relies on the id.","Resolving labels at deploy time and baking the text into the image; the symptom is a label move that changes nothing until the next deploy, and an engineer who concludes the registry is broken.","Logging only the prompt name, not the version and digest; the symptom is an investigation that knows which prompt family failed and cannot tell whether it was before or after Tuesday's change.","Treating the registry as prompt-only and leaving temperature and model id in application config; the symptom is a prompt that passed its eval behaving differently in production because the two were tuned apart.","Allowing the `production` label to be moved from the UI by anyone with edit rights; the symptom is a behaviour change with no pipeline run and no evidence attached."],"when":"Reach for a registry as soon as a second person edits prompts, a prompt needs per-tenant or per-locale\nvariants, or an audit or incident process needs to tie an answer to the exact text that produced it.\nBefore that point a prompts directory in the repository with versioned file names is enough. Measure the\nthings the registry is supposed to deliver: time from prompt edit to production, number of versions with\nattached eval evidence, and whether every production trace resolves to a version. In interviews, the\nexpected depth is the immutable-version versus mutable-label split, why evidence attaches to versions,\nhow the runtime fetch avoids becoming a single point of failure, and why the prompt and its model\nsettings are versioned together.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Integrates the SDK with TTL caching and fallback, logs version and digest on every trace, and makes the eval pipeline run on new versions automatically.","architect":"Decides where the registry lives, who may move which labels, how tenant variants are scoped, and that prompt plus model settings is the versioned unit.","fde":"Uses tenant labels to ship customer-specific prompt variants without forking the product, and shows the customer's change-control team the version history and attached evidence."},"check":{"q":"Your registry stores only prompt text, and application config holds `temperature=0.7`. A platform\nteam migrates the whole fleet to a new model snapshot by changing one config line, and a legal-summary\nprompt that passed its evals last month starts omitting mandatory disclaimers. Explain what the registry\ndesign got wrong and what the version record should have contained.","a":"The registry treated text as the artifact when the artifact is the pair of text and the model\nsettings it was evaluated against. Because the model id lived outside the version, the migration did not\ncreate a new version, triggered no eval, and left every trace pointing at a version id that now means\nsomething different in practice. The version record should hold the model snapshot id, temperature,\nmax tokens and output schema alongside the messages, with a digest over all of it; a model migration\nthen becomes a new version per prompt, each of which must pass the eval suite and be promoted by label.\nThe migration gets slower by exactly the amount of evidence it was skipping, and the disclaimer\nregression would have been caught by the legal-summary golden set before the label moved."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must prompt versions be immutable while labels are mutable?","options":["Immutable versions compress better in storage","Traces must resolve to the exact text forever, while deploy and rollback need to be a pointer move","Labels are immutable too; only drafts can change","Mutable versions would break template rendering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Immutability of content keeps past traces meaningful and evidence attached to the right text; mutability of the pointer is what lets a deploy or rollback happen without shipping code."},{"q":"An application fetches prompts from the registry on every request with no cache. What is the main risk?","options":["Prompts will be rendered with stale variables","Versions will be created on every request","A registry outage or slowdown takes the product down with it","Labels cannot be resolved without a cache"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Runtime fetching is acceptable only when the registry cannot become a single point of failure, which is what the TTL cache, background refresh and bundled fallback provide."},{"q":"Where should eval results for a prompt be attached, and why?","options":["To the label, because that is what production uses","To the version, because evidence describes the content regardless of where it is deployed","To the trace, because that is where the answer appears","To the application release, because prompts ship with code"],"answer":1,"explanation":"\"Was this text tested\" has a single answer per version. Attaching it to a label would lose the evidence every time the label moved and would let an untested version inherit a tested label's history."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-registry","sources":["Official docs: MLflow","Official docs: Hugging Face Hub","Mitchell et al., 2019 — Model Cards for Model Reporting"],"why":"Without a registry, models live as files in a bucket with names like `adapter_final_v2_really.safetensors`.\nNobody can say with confidence which file is serving, a retraining job produces new weights that someone\ncopies into place by hand, rolling back means finding the old file and hoping it is the right one, and\nthe first compliance question, \"which data was this trained on\", has no answer. For LLM applications the\nproblem is sharper because the thing you deploy is rarely one file: it is a base model identity, a LoRA\nadapter, a quantization choice, a tokenizer revision and a serving configuration, and each of these can\ndrift independently while all of them still look like \"the model\".\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. \"Put it in git\" does not survive multi-gigabyte weights\neven with LFS, and git has no notion of lineage to a training run or an evaluation. \"Deploy the newest\nfile in the bucket\" makes deploys non-deterministic: two replicas started an hour apart can serve\ndifferent models. \"Keep a spreadsheet\" is the registry pattern with no enforcement, and it is wrong\nwithin a week.\n\nThe registry fixes this by borrowing the two ideas every package manager already uses: immutable\nversions with content hashes, and mutable named pointers that environments resolve. It then adds the\nthings models need that packages do not: lineage, evaluation evidence, a model card and an approval\nstate.","how":"### Entities\nA **registered model** is a name, such as `support-classifier`. Each **version** is immutable and\nrecords the artifact URI and content hash of the weights, the format or flavour (a Transformers\ncheckpoint, a LoRA adapter, an ONNX graph, a pyfunc wrapper), an input and output signature, the\nparameters and metrics of the run that produced it, lineage (tracking run id, training dataset version,\ncode commit, parent base model and its revision), free-form tags, a description or model card, and an\napproval state. Versions are immutable because a deployment that resolved version 7 must mean the same\nbytes next year, and because evaluation evidence attached to a version is meaningless if the bytes can\nchange underneath it.\n\nAn **alias** is a mutable pointer from a name to a version: `champion`, `challenger`, `staging`,\n`tenant-acme`. MLflow's registry originally used a fixed set of stages (Staging, Production, Archived);\nnewer versions replaced them with free-form aliases plus tags, because fixed stages could not express\n\"two production models for two tenants\" or \"the canary candidate\". Promotion is setting an alias;\nrollback is setting it back to the previous version.\n\n### What counts as a model in an LLM application\nThe registry should hold every artifact whose change alters model behaviour: fine-tuned weights and\nLoRA adapters, quantized variants (a version per quantization, because they are evaluated separately),\nembedding models (whose version must match the vector index built with them), reward or judge models,\nand provider-hosted models. The last case matters: a \"virtual\" version that records only a provider\nsnapshot id and decoding config gives the API-based model the same lifecycle, evidence and rollback\nstory as self-hosted weights. A release bundle then references a model version and a prompt version;\nsome teams register the bundle itself as a model so that one alias move changes both.\n\n### Lifecycle\nRegister from a tracking run, so lineage is automatic. Attach the eval run fingerprint and scores on a\nnamed golden-set version. Request review; the reviewer reads the model card, the diff against the\nchampion's metrics and the slice results. Promote by alias, deploy, observe the canary, promote to\n`champion`. Later, deprecate: mark the version archived, keep the bytes for the retention period the\nlifecycle policy specifies, and refuse new deployments of it.\n\n### Serving integration\nA serving configuration references `models:/support-classifier@champion`. At process start the server\nresolves the alias to a version, downloads the artifact by hash, verifies it, and logs the resolved\nversion into every trace and response header. Resolving once at startup rather than on every request\nis deliberate: a running replica never changes model mid-flight, and a rollout happens by starting new\nreplicas against the new alias target, which is what a canary needs. For embedding models the same\nmechanism prevents the classic bug of querying an index built with embedder v2 using embedder v3: the\nindex build records the embedder version and the query path refuses a mismatch.\n\n### Hugging Face Hub as a registry\nFor open weights the Hub is a git-backed registry: each repository has a commit history, a model card in\nthe README, and `from_pretrained(..., revision=\"<commit sha>\")` pins an exact version. Pinning to a\nbranch name such as `main` is the same mistake as a floating `-latest` alias. Many teams mirror the pinned\nrevision into their own registry so that an upstream deletion or license change cannot break a deploy.\n\n### Governance\nAccess control on alias moves, signed or hash-verified artifacts, a model card per version, and a record\nof who approved what are the parts auditors and security reviewers ask for. They are cheap when designed\nin and expensive to retrofit.","example":"The registry lifecycle with MLflow's client API: register a version from a tracked run, attach the\nevidence a reviewer needs, promote by alias, and have the server resolve the alias once at startup.\nTag values are illustrative; the lineage fields are what matter.\n\n```python\nimport mlflow\nfrom mlflow import MlflowClient\n\nclient = MlflowClient()\nNAME = \"support-classifier\"\n\n# 1. Register the artifact a tracked run produced; the run id is the lineage link to params, data and code\nrun_id = \"3f1c0a9e2b7d\"                                          # the run that trained the LoRA adapter\nmv = mlflow.register_model(f\"runs:/{run_id}/model\", NAME)        # returns a new immutable version number\n\n# 2. Attach the evidence a reviewer and, later, an auditor will ask for\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"base_model\", \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"base_revision\", \"<hf commit sha>\")   # pinned, not 'main'\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"golden_set\", \"golden-v8@5c2e91\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"eval_fingerprint\", \"run:9a71cc\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"eval_accuracy\", \"0.912\")\nclient.update_model_version(NAME, mv.version,\n                            description=\"Retrained on Q3 tickets; fixes refund-intent confusion\")\n\n# 3. Promote by moving a pointer; rollback is this same call with the previous version number\nclient.set_registered_model_alias(NAME, \"challenger\", mv.version)\n# ... canary healthy for the agreed window ...\nclient.set_registered_model_alias(NAME, \"champion\", mv.version)\n\n# 4. The serving process resolves the alias once at startup and records what it got\nresolved = client.get_model_version_by_alias(NAME, \"champion\")\nprint(\"serving\", NAME, \"version\", resolved.version, \"from\", resolved.source)   # log into every trace\nmodel = mlflow.pyfunc.load_model(f\"models:/{NAME}@champion\")\n```","tradeoffs":["Immutable versions plus aliases give deterministic deploys and one-call rollback, at the cost of storage for every version ever promoted; a retention policy matters once weights are tens of gigabytes.","Resolving the alias at startup keeps replicas stable and rollouts explicit, but a hot swap without restart is impossible by design; teams that want instant global switches must accept replicas that disagree for a while.","Registering provider-hosted models as virtual versions gives API models the same lifecycle, but it only helps if application code reads the model id from the registry rather than from its own config.","A full registry with approvals and signing satisfies auditors and security; for a two-person team with one model it is ceremony, and a pinned artifact URI in a config file is the honest minimum.","Registering the whole bundle (model plus prompt) as one versioned unit makes promotion atomic, but it multiplies versions whenever either half changes and blurs which half caused a metric move."],"pitfalls":["Pointing serving config at a branch or `latest` tag instead of a version or alias; the symptom is two replicas returning different answers for the same input after a rolling restart.","Registering weights without the tokenizer and serving config; the symptom is a version that scores well in the eval harness and produces garbage in production because the tokenizer revision differs.","Letting the embedding model version advance without rebuilding the index; the symptom is retrieval quality collapsing after a \"routine\" model upgrade while every service reports healthy.","Attaching eval results to the alias rather than the version; the symptom is a rolled-back champion that inherits the evidence of the version that replaced it.","Mirroring nothing from the Hub and pinning only to a repository name; the symptom is a broken deploy the day the upstream author force-pushes, gates the repository or changes the license."],"when":"Introduce a registry the first time a model is retrained, quantized or swapped after launch, or the\nfirst time two environments need different models; before then a pinned artifact URI with a hash in\nconfig is sufficient and honest. Include provider-hosted models as virtual versions once an API model\nswap would be a user-visible change, which is nearly always. Measure whether every serving replica logs\na resolved version, how long a rollback takes end to end, and whether every promoted version carries\neval evidence and a lineage link. In interviews, expect to explain immutable versions versus aliases,\nwhy aliases are resolved at startup, how the registry prevents an embedder-index mismatch, and how\nprovenance flows from a tracking run into a registered version and out into a trace.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Registers versions from training runs, wires the server to resolve aliases at startup and log the resolved version, and refuses index queries whose embedder version mismatches.","architect":"Defines what counts as a model artifact, the alias vocabulary per environment and tenant, the approval and retention policy, and whether bundles or components are the unit of promotion.","fde":"Keeps a customer-scoped registry or alias set so that a fine-tuned adapter trained on one client's data can never be promoted into another tenant's serving path."},"check":{"q":"A team serves a RAG system with an open embedding model loaded from a Hub repository's `main`\nbranch. One morning retrieval quality drops sharply, no deploy happened, and every health check is\ngreen. Walk through the likely cause and how a registry would have prevented it.","a":"The likely cause is that the upstream repository received a new commit on `main`, so replicas that\nrestarted overnight loaded a different embedding model than the one that built the index; query vectors\nand index vectors now live in different spaces, nearest neighbours are nonsense, and nothing errors\nbecause the shapes still match. A registry prevents this in three places: the embedder is a registered\nversion pinned to a commit hash and mirrored, so a restart loads identical bytes; the index build\nrecords the embedder version it used; and the query path checks that the resolved embedder version\nequals the index's recorded version and refuses to serve, or alerts, on mismatch. The fix is to pin the\nrevision, rebuild or roll back, and add the mismatch check so the failure becomes loud."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a serving process resolve the `champion` alias at startup rather than on each request?","options":["Alias lookups are too slow for request latency budgets","A replica should never change model mid-flight; rollouts happen by starting new replicas, which is what a canary needs","Registries only allow one resolution per client","Per-request resolution would bypass the model cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resolving once makes each replica's behaviour stable and attributable; a change in the alias target becomes a controlled rollout instead of an instantaneous, unobservable fleet-wide switch."},{"q":"What does registering a provider-hosted model as a \"virtual version\" buy you?","options":["Lower API cost through registry-level caching","The ability to fine-tune the provider's weights locally","The same lifecycle, evidence and rollback story for API models as for self-hosted weights","Automatic migration when the provider deprecates a snapshot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The version holds a snapshot id and decoding config, so swapping the API model is a promoted, evaluated change with a rollback, not a config edit nobody can trace."},{"q":"Which lineage field is most important for an auditor asking \"what was this model trained on\"?","options":["The alias the model currently holds","The artifact storage region","The link from the version to the tracking run and its dataset version","The model's parameter count"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lineage flows from the training run, which recorded the dataset version, code commit and params, into the registered version; without that link the question has no verifiable answer."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","sources":["Official docs: MLflow","Official docs: Weights & Biases","Pineau et al., 2021 — Improving Reproducibility in Machine Learning Research"],"why":"Any team that trains, fine-tunes or evaluates models produces runs faster than it can remember them.\nThe first week it is a spreadsheet of learning rates and scores; by the third week the spreadsheet is\nmissing the run everyone is talking about, nobody knows which checkpoint produced the 0.91, and the\nconfig that got it was edited in place since. Fine-tuning makes this combinatorial: LoRA rank, learning\nrate, data mix, epochs and base model revision interact, and a result without its full configuration\ncannot be reproduced or trusted.\n\nLLM application work has the same shape without the training loop. Every evaluation run is an\nexperiment: a prompt version, a model snapshot, a dataset version and a judge configuration go in, and\nscores come out. Teams that keep those results in notebook cells and Slack screenshots cannot answer\n\"did the last three prompt changes help on the refund slice\" without re-running everything.\n\nThe naive alternative, printing to logs and encoding settings in file names, fails because comparison\nand lineage are the whole job: you need to filter a thousand runs by parameter, overlay metric curves,\nand follow a model version back to the run, data and commit that produced it. That is a database with\na specific schema and a UI on top, which is what a tracker is.","how":"### The data model both tools share\nAn **experiment** (MLflow) or **project** (W&B) groups **runs**. A run has **parameters** fixed at start\n(immutable, because changing them mid-run would make comparisons meaningless), **metrics** logged as time\nseries against a step or epoch (so you can see divergence or over-fitting at step *k*, not just the\nfinal number), **artifacts** which are files (checkpoints, per-item eval outputs, plots, configs),\n**tags** for free-form filtering, and automatically captured context: git commit, command line, Python\nenvironment, and system metrics such as GPU utilisation. Runs can be nested for hyperparameter sweeps.\nEverything is queryable, so \"all runs where `lora_r=16` sorted by `eval_acc`\" is one call.\n\n### MLflow\nMLflow is Apache-licensed open source that started at Databricks. Its tracking server has a backend\nstore (a SQL database for runs, params and metrics) and an artifact store (local disk, S3, GCS or Azure\nBlob for files), which is why self-hosting is a container plus a database plus a bucket. Around tracking\nit adds a model registry with versions and aliases, a packaging format with \"flavours\" (`pyfunc`,\n`transformers`, `sklearn` and others) that lets any registered model be loaded with one call, and\nautologging integrations that capture params and metrics from common training frameworks without\nexplicit calls. The 3.x line added GenAI features — tracing, an evaluation API and a prompt registry —\nso an LLM team can keep evals and prompts in the same store as training runs. Databricks offers a\nmanaged version; everything else is self-hosted.\n\n### Weights & Biases\nW&B is SaaS-first with a self-hosted server option for data-residency requirements. The API is\n`wandb.init(project, config)`, `wandb.log({...}, step=...)` and `run.finish()`. Its distinguishing\npieces are **Artifacts**, versioned datasets and models with an automatic lineage graph (which run\nconsumed which artifact version and produced which), **Sweeps**, a built-in hyperparameter search\norchestrator that schedules agents across machines, **Tables** for per-example analysis inside the UI,\n**Reports** for sharing, and **Weave**, its toolkit for tracing and evaluating LLM applications. The UI\nis the usual reason teams pick it; the usual reasons they do not are cost per seat and the fact that\nmetrics and artifacts leave the network unless you self-host.\n\n### How an LLM team uses either\nA fine-tuning run logs `base_model`, `base_revision`, `lora_r`, `lr`, `train_set` version hash and\n`git_sha` as params, train and eval loss per step as metrics, the adapter directory and the per-item\neval outputs as artifacts, then registers the adapter as a model version so lineage flows into the\nregistry. An evaluation run logs prompt version, model snapshot, dataset hash and judge config as\nparams, aggregate and per-slice scores as metrics, and the per-item outputs file as an artifact so error\nanalysis can start from the run page. The tracker becomes the place where \"what did we try and what\nhappened\" is answered.\n\n### Choosing\nData residency or regulated environments push toward self-hosted MLflow or a W&B server. A team already\non Databricks gets MLflow for free. A research-heavy team that runs sweeps daily usually prefers W&B's\nUI and sweep orchestration. LLM application teams increasingly keep traces and evals in an observability\nplatform such as Langfuse or LangSmith and use the tracker only for training; the important decision is\none system of record per artifact class, not one tool for everything.","example":"The same LoRA fine-tuning run logged two ways. Parameters are fixed at the start, metrics are\nstep-indexed, artifacts are files, and the dataset and code versions are logged as params so the run is\nreproducible. `train_and_eval` is your loop, yielding per-step metrics.\n\n```python\nimport mlflow, wandb, subprocess\n\ncfg = {\"base\": \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\", \"base_revision\": \"<hf commit sha>\",\n       \"lora_r\": 16, \"lr\": 2e-4, \"epochs\": 2,\n       \"train_set\": \"tickets-v3@9c1e04\", \"golden_set\": \"golden-v8@5c2e91\"}\ncfg[\"git_sha\"] = subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"]).decode().strip()\n\n# --- MLflow: self-hosted tracking server; runs grouped by experiment -------------------------\nmlflow.set_tracking_uri(\"http://mlflow.internal:5000\")\nmlflow.set_experiment(\"support-classifier\")\nwith mlflow.start_run(run_name=\"lora-r16-lr2e-4\") as run:\n    mlflow.log_params(cfg)                                            # immutable for the run's lifetime\n    for step, (train_loss, eval_acc) in enumerate(train_and_eval(cfg)):\n        mlflow.log_metrics({\"train_loss\": train_loss, \"eval_acc\": eval_acc}, step=step)\n    mlflow.log_artifact(\"outputs/eval_items.jsonl\")                   # per-item outputs for error analysis\n    mlflow.log_artifacts(\"outputs/adapter\", artifact_path=\"model\")    # the thing the registry will version\n    print(\"register with run id:\", run.info.run_id)\n\n# --- W&B: SaaS or self-hosted server; config + log + a versioned Artifact with lineage --------\nrun = wandb.init(project=\"support-classifier\", name=\"lora-r16-lr2e-4\", config=cfg)\nfor step, (train_loss, eval_acc) in enumerate(train_and_eval(cfg)):\n    wandb.log({\"train_loss\": train_loss, \"eval_acc\": eval_acc}, step=step)\nart = wandb.Artifact(\"support-classifier-adapter\", type=\"model\", metadata=cfg)\nart.add_dir(\"outputs/adapter\")\nart.add_file(\"outputs/eval_items.jsonl\")\nrun.log_artifact(art)                                                 # becomes v0, v1, ... in the lineage graph\nrun.finish()\n```","tradeoffs":["Self-hosted MLflow keeps data in your network and costs nothing in licences, but you operate a server, a database and an artifact store, and the UI is thinner than W&B's.","W&B's hosted UI, sweeps and lineage graph save engineering time, but per-seat pricing and data leaving the network flip the decision in regulated environments unless you run the self-hosted server.","Logging every per-item output as an artifact makes error analysis one click away, at the cost of storage that grows with runs times dataset size; sample or compress once eval sets reach six figures.","Autologging captures params and metrics with zero code, but it also captures whatever the framework decides is interesting, which can bury the three numbers you care about under three hundred.","Keeping LLM eval runs in the training tracker unifies history, whereas keeping them in the observability platform links them to traces; the split that wins depends on whether your evals start from production traces or from training jobs."],"pitfalls":["Logging the final metric only; the symptom is a run that looks best on its last value while its curve shows it diverged and recovered by luck, which the next seed will not repeat.","Forgetting the data version and git commit in params; the symptom is a top run that nobody can reproduce because the training set was regenerated since.","Mutating a config dict after `log_params` and assuming the tracker noticed; the symptom is runs whose recorded learning rate differs from the one actually used.","Treating the tracker as a backup for weights; the symptom is a bucket bill that doubles monthly and a cleanup that deletes the one checkpoint the registry still points to.","Running evals outside the tracker \"because it is just a prompt change\"; the symptom is a team arguing from memory about whether version 11 or 12 did better on the hard slice."],"when":"Use a tracker from the first fine-tuning run or the first eval you intend to compare against a later\none, which is essentially always. Choose MLflow when you need self-hosting, already run Databricks, or\nwant the registry and model packaging in the same system; choose W&B when sweep orchestration, the UI\nand artifact lineage are worth the subscription and data can leave your network or you will run the\nself-hosted server. Measure whether every registered model version links to a run, whether every run\nhas a data version and commit, and how long it takes a new team member to find \"the best run on the\nrefund slice\". In interviews, be ready to describe the run data model, how lineage flows from a run into\nthe registry, and the hosting and ecosystem differences between the two tools.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments training and eval scripts with params, step metrics and artifacts, and registers the resulting model version from the run so lineage is automatic.","architect":"Chooses the system of record per artifact class, decides self-hosted versus SaaS from residency and cost, and sets the minimum metadata every run must carry.","fde":"Stands up a tracker inside the customer's environment when their data cannot leave, so customer-specific fine-tuning and evals have the same history and reproducibility as internal work."},"check":{"q":"A colleague reports that run 42 reached 0.91 accuracy and asks you to promote its adapter. The\nrun page shows only `lr` and `epochs` as params and a single final metric. What questions do you ask\nbefore promoting, and what should the run have logged to make them unnecessary?","a":"First, which base model revision, which training set version and which code commit produced it;\nwithout those the adapter cannot be retrained or audited. Second, what the evaluation set was and\nwhether it was the current golden version, because 0.91 on last month's set is not comparable to this\nweek's baseline. Third, how the metric evolved over steps, since a single final number can hide a\ndivergent run that landed on a good value by chance. Fourth, where the per-item outputs are, so the\nrefund-intent slice can be checked. The run should have logged base revision, dataset hashes, git sha\nand LoRA settings as params, train and eval metrics per step, and the eval outputs and adapter as\nartifacts, with the adapter registered from the run so the registry version carries that lineage."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are a run's parameters fixed at the start while metrics are logged over time?","options":["Parameters are stored in a different database table for speed","Parameters define the experiment and must stay constant for comparison, while metrics describe how it unfolded","Metrics cannot be stored as strings","Parameters are only used by sweeps"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A run is an answer to \"what happens with this configuration\"; if the configuration changes mid-run the question changes. Step-indexed metrics let you see divergence and over-fitting instead of a final number."},{"q":"Which difference most often decides between MLflow and W&B in a regulated enterprise?","options":["W&B cannot log artifacts","MLflow has no model registry","Self-hosting and data residency: MLflow is self-hosted by default, W&B is SaaS-first with a self-hosted option","MLflow only supports PyTorch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both tools cover the core data model. Where metrics, configs and artifacts are allowed to live is usually the constraint that decides, with W&B's UI and sweeps as the counterweight when residency is not binding."},{"q":"What links a registered model version back to the data it was trained on?","options":["The alias assigned at promotion time","The run that produced it, which logged the dataset version as a parameter","The artifact file's modification timestamp","The model card's free-text description"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lineage is captured at training time in the run; registering from the run carries that link into the version. An alias says where a model is deployed, not where it came from."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","sources":["Kohavi, Tang & Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery"],"why":"A change to an AI system is non-local. Swapping the model, editing three lines of the system prompt or\ntightening a tool schema changes behaviour on *every* request, including the long tail your golden set\nnever sampled: odd phrasings, a language you did not test, tool outputs that are longer than expected,\na tenant whose documents are all scanned PDFs. A conventional service change touches one code path and\na unit test proves it; an LLM change touches a probability distribution and only traffic reveals it.\n\nThe naive approach is \"the eval gate passed, deploy to 100%\". It fails for three reasons. The golden\nset is small and ages: it reflects what users asked when it was built. Judge-based scores are noisy, so\na two-point gain on 300 cases is often within noise. And some regressions are invisible offline because\nthey depend on production conditions: cold prompt caches, rate limits on the new model, latency under\nreal concurrency, or a tool that behaves differently with real credentials. A big-bang deploy puts the\nwhole user base inside the blast radius for the hours it takes a quality regression to show up in\nfeedback.\n\nShadow and canary deployments exist to buy information about live behaviour at a bounded cost. They\ndiffer in what they buy: shadow buys a like-for-like comparison with no exposure but no user feedback;\ncanary buys real user outcomes at the price of real exposure.","how":"### What a \"version\" is\nThe thing you roll out is a bundle, not a model: model id (pinned snapshot), prompt template version,\ndecoding parameters, tool schemas, retrieval index version and guardrail configuration. Both patterns\nneed the bundle to be addressable as one immutable unit, because a canary that mixes a new prompt with\nan old index is testing a combination you never evaluated and cannot roll back to.\n\n### Shadow (traffic mirroring)\n1. The gateway receives a request and serves it with the incumbent as usual.\n2. It forks a copy to the candidate asynchronously, after the user response is on its way, so the\n   user never waits on the candidate.\n3. Both outputs are logged under the same trace id along with latency, tokens, cost, schema validity,\n   refusal flags and tool calls.\n4. An offline comparison job scores pairs: deterministic checks on 100% of pairs, LLM-judge pairwise\n   preference on a sample, and disagreement analysis (where do the two differ most, by intent or tenant).\n\nShadow is safe *because* nothing the candidate produces leaves the system. That same property is its\nlimit. Side-effecting tools must be stubbed or sandboxed, because an agent in shadow must not send the\nemail twice. Multi-turn effects are invisible, because the user's next message responds to the\nincumbent's answer, not the candidate's. And there is no user feedback at all, so shadow answers \"is it\ndifferent and is it cheaper or slower\", not \"do users prefer it\". The mirrored slice also doubles\ninference cost on that slice, so most teams shadow 5–20% of traffic (illustrative) or shadow 100% for a\nshort window.\n\n### Canary (partial live exposure)\n1. The router assigns each request to a bucket by hashing a stable key, usually user or session id, and\n   routes buckets below the canary percentage to the candidate. Hashing *because* a conversation must not\n   flip models mid-thread; random per-request assignment produces incoherent sessions and contaminates\n   the comparison.\n2. Guard metrics are watched on both arms over a bake window: error rate, p95 latency, schema-failure\n   rate, refusal rate, cost per request, tool-failure rate, and whatever quality proxy you trust\n   (thumbs-down rate, escalation rate, judge score on a sample).\n3. The percentage ramps in steps, for example 1% → 5% → 25% → 50% → 100%, each step held long enough to\n   collect a minimum sample. Small first steps *because* the point of the first step is to catch\n   catastrophic breakage (wrong schema, 429s from the new model's quota) with the fewest users exposed.\n4. Any guard breach triggers automatic rollback of the pointer to the incumbent; promotion to 100%\n   requires that the success metrics are not worse, not merely that nothing exploded.\n\n### What the numbers can and cannot tell you\nDetecting a shift in a rate of δ needs sample size roughly proportional to 1/δ². A 1% canary on a\nservice doing 20,000 requests a day gives 200 canary requests a day; that is enough to notice a\nschema-failure rate jumping from 0.5% to 5%, and nowhere near enough to notice a judge score moving\nfrom 4.1 to 4.0. So the early steps are for catastrophic signals, and the later, larger steps are where\nquality comparisons become meaningful. Treat the canary as a controlled experiment only when the two\narms are assigned randomly and compared on the same window; comparing this week's canary to last\nweek's baseline confounds the version with time.\n\n### Where it lives\nRouting, hashing, mirroring and the rollout percentage belong in the AI gateway or control plane as\nconfiguration, not in application code, so a rollout or rollback is a config change with no deploy.","example":"A minimal gateway router: sticky canary assignment by user hash, an asynchronous shadow fork for a\nseparate candidate, and paired logging. The shadow call runs after the response is returned, and the\nshadow candidate is configured with side-effecting tools disabled.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, hashlib, json, time\n\nROLLOUT = {\"incumbent\": \"bundle-v12\", \"canary\": \"bundle-v13\", \"canary_pct\": 5,\n           \"shadow\": \"bundle-v14\", \"shadow_pct\": 20}\n\ndef bucket(key: str) -> int:                       # stable 0..99 bucket per user\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100\n\nasync def call_bundle(bundle: str, req: dict, allow_side_effects=True) -> dict:\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    out = await llm_app.run(bundle, req, allow_side_effects=allow_side_effects)\n    return {\"bundle\": bundle, \"text\": out.text, \"schema_ok\": out.schema_ok,\n            \"refused\": out.refused, \"cost\": out.cost, \"ms\": (time.perf_counter() - t) * 1e3}\n\nasync def shadow(req: dict, served: dict, trace_id: str):\n    cand = await call_bundle(ROLLOUT[\"shadow\"], req, allow_side_effects=False)\n    log.info(json.dumps({\"trace\": trace_id, \"served\": served, \"shadow\": cand}))\n\nasync def handle(req: dict, user_id: str, trace_id: str) -> dict:\n    b = bucket(user_id)\n    bundle = ROLLOUT[\"canary\"] if b < ROLLOUT[\"canary_pct\"] else ROLLOUT[\"incumbent\"]\n    served = await call_bundle(bundle, req)\n    metrics.observe(bundle=bundle, ms=served[\"ms\"], schema_ok=served[\"schema_ok\"],\n                    refused=served[\"refused\"], cost=served[\"cost\"])\n    if b < ROLLOUT[\"shadow_pct\"]:\n        asyncio.create_task(shadow(req, served, trace_id))   # never on the user's critical path\n    return served\n# ROLLOUT is loaded from the control plane; promoting or rolling back is a config write, not a deploy.\n```","tradeoffs":["Shadow gives a like-for-like comparison with zero user exposure, but you pay roughly double inference on the mirrored slice and learn nothing about user preference or multi-turn behaviour.","Canary gives real outcomes, but every percent of exposure is real users seeing a possibly worse system; once the change is low-risk and well-evaluated offline, the extra caution costs more than it saves.","Sticky (hashed) assignment keeps conversations coherent and the experiment clean, but it means a bad canary hits the same users repeatedly, so a 5% canary is a 100% regression for those users.","Small early steps limit blast radius but slow the rollout; for a change with a hard deadline (a vendor deprecation date) you may have to accept larger steps and lean on shadow results instead.","Automatic rollback on guard metrics removes humans from the loop at 3 a.m., but with noisy quality metrics and small samples it will roll back good releases unless thresholds carry a minimum sample."],"pitfalls":["Shadowing an agent with live tools; the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or payments appearing with no user action behind them.","Random per-request routing instead of per-session hashing; the symptom is users reporting that the assistant \"changed personality\" mid-conversation and metrics that look fine in aggregate.","Comparing the canary to a historical baseline rather than a concurrent control; the symptom is a \"regression\" that disappears when you notice the canary window included a product launch.","Judging the canary on a quality score after 150 samples; the symptom is rollbacks and promotions that flip from day to day with no change in the system.","Ignoring warm-up effects: the candidate model has a cold prompt cache and a fresh rate-limit bucket; the symptom is a latency and 429 spike in the first minutes that disappears, then gets blamed on the version.","Canarying only internal users; the symptom is a clean canary followed by a wave of complaints from the customer segment that writes in a different language or uploads different documents."],"when":"Shadow first when the change is large or structurally different: a new model family, a new provider, a\nrewritten agent loop, a new retrieval pipeline. Its output tells you whether the candidate is safe\nenough to put in front of anyone and where it disagrees with the incumbent, which is where to look. Move\nto a canary when you need user outcomes to decide, or when the change is incremental and offline evals\nalready pass. Skip both for trivial, fully reversible changes with strong offline coverage, where the\ncost of the process exceeds the risk. Measure guard metrics on both arms, demand a minimum sample before\nany quality verdict, and automate the rollback path before the first ramp. In interviews, the expected\ndepth is to explain why sticky assignment matters, why shadow cannot test multi-turn agents, and how\nsample size limits what a 1% canary can detect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the hashed router and asynchronous mirroring in the gateway, tags every trace with the bundle id, and builds the paired-comparison job that scores shadow output.","architect":"Decides what the deployable bundle is, where rollout state lives (control plane, not code) and which guard metrics gate each ramp step for which risk class of change.","fde":"Runs shadow mode against a customer's live traffic before cutover so the customer sees a disagreement report instead of a surprise, and negotiates which users form the first canary cohort."},"check":{"q":"You are migrating a customer-support agent from model A to model B. The agent can issue refunds.\nOffline evals show B slightly ahead. Design the rollout and justify each step.","a":"Start with shadow at a meaningful slice of traffic with the refund tool disabled for the shadow\narm, because a side-effecting tool cannot be mirrored safely. Score pairs on deterministic checks\n(schema validity, refusal rate, tool-call mix, latency, cost) at 100% and judge preference on a sample;\nanalyse where B disagrees most with A by intent. If nothing is alarming, canary B to about 1% of\nsessions by user hash, with automatic rollback on error rate, schema failures, latency and\nrefund-rate anomalies; hold until a minimum sample accrues, then ramp through 5, 25 and 50%. Use\nthumbs-down and escalation rate as success metrics at the larger steps, where samples allow it. Keep\nA's bundle warm and the routing in control-plane config so rollback is a pointer flip, and note that\nB's refund behaviour in canary is the one thing shadow could not test, so it gets the tightest alert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a shadow deployment unable to evaluate a multi-turn conversational agent properly?","options":["Because shadow traffic cannot be logged alongside the served response","Because the user's next turn responds to the incumbent's answer, so the candidate never gets the conversation it would have produced","Because shadow deployments only work for stateless classification models","Because the candidate's latency makes the mirrored copy time out"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shadow forks the same input to both versions, but a conversation diverges after the first reply. The candidate sees follow-ups shaped by the incumbent, so its multi-turn behaviour is never observed."},{"q":"A team runs a 1% canary on a service with 10,000 requests per day and wants to know within a day whether the judge score moved from 4.2 to 4.1. What is the right assessment?","options":["Sufficient: 100 samples is enough for any metric","Sufficient, provided the judge runs at temperature zero","Insufficient: detecting a small shift in a noisy score needs far more samples; the 1% step is for catching catastrophic failures","Insufficient only because the canary should run for a week regardless of traffic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sample size needed grows roughly with the inverse square of the effect size. A 100-request slice can reveal a schema-failure spike but cannot resolve a 0.1-point judge difference; use larger ramp steps for quality verdicts."},{"q":"Why should canary assignment hash on user or session id rather than pick randomly per request?","options":["Hashing is faster than random number generation at gateway scale","Random assignment would exceed the canary percentage over time","Per-session stickiness keeps conversations coherent and prevents the arms from contaminating each other's results","Hashing lets the canary cohort be chosen from internal users only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If consecutive turns of one conversation hit different versions, users see inconsistent behaviour and each arm's metrics include context produced by the other arm, which invalidates the comparison."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/rollback","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery"],"why":"For a stateless web service, rollback is a solved problem: redeploy the previous container image. AI\nsystems break that model in three ways.\n\nFirst, the deployable unit is a bundle of artefacts with different lifecycles and different stores. The\nprompt lives in a registry, the model behind a provider API or a GPU fleet, the retrieval index in a\nvector database, the tool schemas in code, the guardrail policy in a config service. Rolling back one\nof them without the others yields a combination that was never evaluated: yesterday's prompt against\ntoday's model is a new version, not the old one.\n\nSecond, some changes destroy their own undo path. Re-embedding a corpus in place with a new embedding\nmodel overwrites the vectors the old prompt was tuned against. Replacing a fine-tuned model on a\nprovider that keeps only the latest job leaves nothing to return to. A vendor retiring the previous\nsnapshot removes the old model entirely.\n\nThird, the failures that justify a rollback are usually quality regressions, visible only through\nlagging signals (thumbs-down rate, escalations, judge scores), not through a crash. By the time a human\nnotices, the bad version has been serving for hours. The naive alternative, \"just tweak the prompt to\nfix it\", compounds the problem: every edit under pressure is another unevaluated version, and the\noriginal cause is now buried under three patches. Rollback exists so that the first move in an incident\nis a known-good state, after which diagnosis can happen calmly.","how":"### Make versions immutable and deployment a pointer\nEvery release is a manifest that pins each component: model id with snapshot date, prompt version hash,\ndecoding parameters, tool schema version, index id, guardrail policy version, and the feature flags\nthat were on. Manifests are immutable and stored in the registry. The environment, say `prod`, is an\nalias pointing at one manifest. Deploying is moving the alias forward; rolling back is moving it to the\nprevious manifest. This is O(1) *because* nothing is rebuilt, re-embedded or re-trained; only a pointer\nchanges, and the data plane picks up the new pointer on its next config refresh.\n\nThe rule that follows: you may only roll back to a manifest that has previously served production (or\npassed the same gates). Hand-assembling \"old prompt + new model\" during an incident is a forward change\nin disguise.\n\n### Keep the previous version reachable\nA pointer is only useful if its target still exists and is warm.\n- Self-hosted models: keep the previous weights loaded or at least on local disk; loading tens of\n  gigabytes from object storage can take minutes (illustrative), which is the whole rollback budget.\n- Provider models: pin snapshots, and track the vendor's deprecation calendar so the \"previous\" model is\n  not one that was retired last month.\n- Indexes: build the new index alongside the old (blue/green), switch the alias, keep the old index\n  until the new one has baked. Never re-embed in place.\n- Fine-tunes: keep the previous adapter or checkpoint and the training data that produced it.\n\n### Decide triggers before the release\nRollback should be a decision made in advance and executed by a rule, not debated in the incident\nchannel. Guard metrics with thresholds and windows (error rate, p95 latency, schema-failure rate,\nrefusal rate, cost per request, tool-failure rate) trigger automatically; quality signals (judge score\non a sample, thumbs-down rate, escalation rate) usually trigger a human decision because they are\nnoisier and slower. Each trigger carries a minimum sample size, *because* a threshold evaluated on\ntwenty requests produces flapping.\n\n### Handle what a pointer flip does not restore\nRollback restores behaviour, not data. Things to check after the flip:\n- Caches keyed without the version: a semantic or response cache filled by the bad version will keep\n  serving its answers. Key caches by manifest id, or purge on rollback.\n- Conversations in flight: a session whose earlier turns and compaction summaries were produced by the\n  bad version continues under the old one; usually acceptable, but worth knowing when reading feedback.\n- Agent memory and side effects: notes the bad version wrote to long-term memory, tickets it filed,\n  emails it sent. Rollback does not undo them; the audit log identifies them for cleanup.\n- Downstream consumers: if another team cached embeddings or outputs from the bad version, tell them.\n\n### Rehearse it\nA rollback that has never been executed is a hypothesis. Exercise it in CI (deploy v2, roll back to\nv1, run a smoke eval) and in periodic game days, and time it against the MTTR target in the runbook.","example":"A registry with immutable manifests, an environment alias, and a guard loop that rolls back when a\nmetric breaches its threshold with enough samples. Promotion records history so rollback has a\nvalidated target.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Manifest:\n    id: str; model: str; prompt: str; params: dict; tools: str; index: str; guardrails: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass Registry:\n    manifests: dict = field(default_factory=dict)\n    alias: dict = field(default_factory=dict)      # env -> manifest id\n    history: dict = field(default_factory=dict)    # env -> [manifest ids that served]\n\n    def promote(self, env: str, mid: str):\n        assert mid in self.manifests, \"unknown manifest\"\n        self.history.setdefault(env, []).append(mid)\n        self.alias[env] = mid                      # O(1): a pointer move, nothing rebuilt\n        cache.invalidate(namespace=env)            # cached answers belonged to the old version\n\n    def rollback(self, env: str) -> str:\n        served = self.history[env]\n        assert len(served) >= 2, \"nothing validated to roll back to\"\n        served.pop()                               # drop the bad one\n        self.alias[env] = served[-1]\n        cache.invalidate(namespace=env)\n        return served[-1]\n\nGUARDS = {\"schema_fail_rate\": (0.02, 200), \"p95_ms\": (6000, 200), \"refusal_rate\": (0.10, 200)}\n\ndef check_guards(reg: Registry, env: str, window: dict) -> str | None:\n    for metric, (limit, min_n) in GUARDS.items():\n        if window[\"n\"] >= min_n and window[metric] > limit:\n            target = reg.rollback(env)\n            alerts.page(f\"{env}: {metric}={window[metric]:.3f} > {limit}; rolled back to {target}\")\n            return target\n    return None\n```","tradeoffs":["Keeping the previous model warm makes rollback instant but costs GPU memory or a second serving pool; for infrequent releases a slower cold rollback may be the cheaper choice.","Automatic rollback shortens time-to-recover at night but, if thresholds lack minimum samples or metrics are noisy, it rolls back healthy releases and trains people to ignore it.","Bundle-level rollback is safe because the target was validated, but it is coarse: it reverts an unrelated good change that shipped in the same manifest, which argues for small manifests.","Blue/green indexes make retrieval changes reversible at the cost of double storage during the bake window; for very large corpora that cost can dominate and force a partial-rebuild strategy.","Immutable manifests and aliases impose registry discipline on every team; the overhead is real but it is what makes \"which version served this request\" answerable at all."],"pitfalls":["Re-embedding the corpus in place; the symptom is a retrieval regression after a model change with no way back except a multi-hour rebuild.","Caches not keyed by version; the symptom is \"we rolled back but users still see the bad answers\" for as long as the cache TTL.","Relying on the vendor to keep serving the previous model; the symptom is a rollback target that returns a model-not-found error during the incident.","Rolling back only the prompt because \"the model did not change\", when the model alias silently updated underneath; the symptom is a rollback that does not restore the old behaviour.","Never rehearsing; the symptom is a rollback runbook that fails on a permissions error at the exact moment it is needed.","Treating rollback as the whole fix; the symptom is memory entries, tickets or emails created by the bad version surfacing days later because nobody reconciled the audit log."],"when":"Rollback is the first move whenever a release correlates with a guard breach or a credible quality\nregression, and diagnosis comes second; the cost of being wrong about a rollback is small if the\nprevious version was validated, while the cost of debugging live is paid by users. Prefer fixing\nforward only when the previous version is itself broken (a vendor retired it, an index was overwritten)\nor when the fault is clearly outside the release. Measure time-to-rollback in rehearsals, keep it in\nminutes, and make the trigger list part of every release plan. In day-to-day work this looks like\nmanifests in a registry, aliases in a control plane and a rollback button that engineers trust. In\ninterviews, the strong answer explains why an AI rollback is harder than a container rollback and what\nhas to be true beforehand for a pointer flip to actually restore behaviour.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds releases as immutable manifests, keys caches by manifest id, keeps the previous weights or index reachable, and wires guard metrics to the rollback action.","architect":"Defines the deployable unit, the retention policy for previous versions (how many, how warm), and which triggers are automatic versus human, balancing recovery time against cost.","fde":"Agrees rollback triggers and the rollback runbook with the customer before go-live, and verifies in the customer's environment that the previous version is actually still deployable."},"check":{"q":"A release changed the prompt and switched the embedding model, re-indexing the corpus in place.\nTwo days later answer quality drops. Walk through what rollback can and cannot do here and what you\nwould change for next time.","a":"The prompt can be rolled back instantly if it is versioned in a registry and the environment\nalias can be repointed. The index cannot: re-embedding in place destroyed the old vectors, so the\nold prompt would now run against a corpus embedded by the new model, which is a combination never\nevaluated and possibly worse. The honest options are to rebuild the old index from source (hours),\nor to keep the new index and fix the prompt forward with a proper canary. For next time: treat the\nbundle as one manifest, build the new index blue/green beside the old and switch an alias, keep the old\nindex through a bake window, key any response cache by manifest id, and define the quality trigger\nand minimum sample before release so the decision happens on day one rather than day two."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"roll back the prompt but keep the new model\" risky even when the model change looked harmless?","options":["Prompt registries cannot store more than one version per model","It produces a combination that was never evaluated, so it is a forward change rather than a return to a known-good state","Models cache the prompt, so the old prompt will not take effect","It doubles inference cost during the transition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rollback is only safe because its target previously passed gates and served traffic. Mixing components from different manifests creates a new, unvalidated version under incident pressure."},{"q":"After rolling back, users keep receiving the bad version's answers for an hour. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The rollback pointer takes an hour to propagate through DNS","The old model needs an hour to warm its KV cache","A response or semantic cache was keyed without the version id, so it still serves answers produced by the bad version","The guard metrics window is one hour long"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rollback restores the code path, not the data it produced. A cache filled by the bad version keeps serving until its TTL unless caches are keyed by manifest or purged on rollback."},{"q":"Which practice most directly ensures that a retrieval-index change can be rolled back?","options":["Storing the embedding model name in the prompt","Lowering the index's HNSW parameters so rebuilds are faster","Building the new index beside the old one and switching an alias, keeping the old index during a bake window","Re-embedding only documents that changed since the last release"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blue/green indexing preserves the old vectors, so rollback is an alias switch. In-place re-embedding overwrites them and turns rollback into a multi-hour rebuild."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-monitoring","sources":["Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"Service monitoring assumes failures are visible: a request errors, times out or is slow. An LLM\napplication fails differently. The model answers confidently with the wrong refund policy; the\nretriever returns last year's price list; the agent stops calling the search tool because the vendor\nquietly updated the model and it now prefers to answer from memory. All of these are HTTP 200, p95\nwithin budget, dashboards green.\n\nGround truth is also late or absent. A classifier in a bank gets labels when the loan defaults, months\nlater; a support assistant never learns whether the customer's problem was solved unless you build the\nloop. So you cannot monitor \"accuracy\" directly the way you monitor error rate.\n\nThe naive alternatives each cover one corner. \"We have evals in CI\" measures a fixed set at release\ntime; production is a different distribution and it changes after release, while the model itself can\nchange underneath a provider alias. \"We watch user complaints\" is sparse, biased toward the angriest\nusers and days late. \"We log everything\" produces terabytes nobody reads. Monitoring exists to make\nthe question \"is the system behaving as it did when we validated it?\" answerable continuously, cheaply\nand per segment, with a path from any alert to the concrete requests behind it.","how":"### Layer 1: operational signals\nThe ordinary SRE set, tagged with AI-specific dimensions: request rate, error rate by class (provider\n429s, 5xxs, timeouts, guardrail blocks), latency as time-to-first-token and total, input and output\ntokens, cost per request, tool-call failures and retries. Standard plumbing, but every series must\ncarry model id, prompt version, bundle id and tenant as labels, *because* the first question after any\nanomaly is \"which version, which customer\".\n\n### Layer 2: behavioural proxies (no labels needed)\nCheap functions of the output computed on 100% of traffic: refusal rate (a small classifier or pattern\nmatch), schema-validation failure rate for structured outputs, output length distribution, empty or\ntruncated responses (finish reason), repetition, language mismatch between input and output, tool-call\nrate and which tools, citation presence in RAG answers, retrieval score distribution, \"I don't know\"\nrate. None of these is quality. Each is a proxy whose *shift* is informative: if the refusal rate\ndoubles after a model update, something changed, and you know within minutes rather than after the\nweekly feedback review. Proxies are chosen *because* they are deterministic, free, and move early.\n\n### Layer 3: quality and outcome signals\n- Online judge: an LLM scores a sample of live responses against a rubric (groundedness, completeness,\n  policy adherence). Sampled *because* a judge call costs about as much as the request it scores; 1–5%\n  is a common order of magnitude (illustrative), stratified so small tenants are still covered.\n- Human review queues: low-judge-score or flagged traces routed to reviewers; the labels feed both the\n  monitor's calibration and the golden set.\n- Explicit feedback: thumbs, ratings, \"report\". Sparse and selection-biased, so track the rate of\n  negative feedback per hundred responses rather than its raw count.\n- Implicit feedback: regenerate, copy, abandon, escalate to human, task completion. Higher volume, less\n  precise; correlate once with human labels to learn what each means.\n- Business outcomes: resolution rate, conversion, handle time. The slowest signal and the one leaders\n  believe.\n\nEach signal has a label latency: implicit feedback in seconds, explicit in minutes, judge in minutes to\nhours, business metrics in days. Monitoring design is largely about using fast proxies to decide where\nto spend slow, expensive labels.\n\n### Slicing, baselines and alerting\nAggregates hide regressions. A 2% drop in overall judge score can be a 40% drop for one tenant or one\nintent. Slice every metric by version, tenant, intent class, language and input length bucket. Compare\nagainst a baseline that controls for time: the same hour last week, or the incumbent version in a\nconcurrent canary. Alert on sudden shifts and on slow trends separately, and require a minimum sample\nper window *because* judged metrics on thirty requests are noise. Route operational breaches to the\npager and quality shifts to a ticket with the top disagreeing examples attached.\n\n### Close the loop\nEvery metric should be derivable from traces so an alert can drill into the requests behind it, and\nevery reviewed failure should become a golden-set case. Monitoring that does not feed the eval suite\nfinds the same regression twice.","example":"A windowed monitor over logged events: proxies on all requests, judge scores on the sampled subset,\ncompared per version against a baseline window with a minimum-sample guard.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom statistics import mean\n\nMIN_N = 200                       # no verdicts on tiny windows\nLIMITS = {\"refusal_rate\": 0.05, \"schema_fail_rate\": 0.02, \"judge_drop\": 0.3}\n\ndef summarise(events):            # events: dicts from the trace store, one per request\n    by = defaultdict(list)\n    for e in events:\n        by[(e[\"bundle\"], e[\"tenant\"])].append(e)\n    out = {}\n    for key, es in by.items():\n        judged = [e[\"judge\"] for e in es if e.get(\"judge\") is not None]   # sampled subset\n        out[key] = {\"n\": len(es),\n                    \"refusal_rate\": mean(e[\"refused\"] for e in es),\n                    \"schema_fail_rate\": mean(not e[\"schema_ok\"] for e in es),\n                    \"p95_ms\": sorted(e[\"ms\"] for e in es)[int(0.95 * len(es)) - 1],\n                    \"cost_per_req\": mean(e[\"cost\"] for e in es),\n                    \"judge_mean\": mean(judged) if judged else None, \"judge_n\": len(judged)}\n    return out\n\ndef alerts(current, baseline):\n    found = []\n    for key, c in current.items():\n        if c[\"n\"] < MIN_N:\n            continue\n        b = baseline.get(key)\n        for m in (\"refusal_rate\", \"schema_fail_rate\"):\n            if c[m] > LIMITS[m] and (b is None or c[m] > 2 * b[m]):\n                found.append((key, m, c[m], \"page\"))\n        if b and c[\"judge_n\"] >= 50 and b[\"judge_n\"] >= 50 \\\n                and b[\"judge_mean\"] - c[\"judge_mean\"] > LIMITS[\"judge_drop\"]:\n            found.append((key, \"judge_mean\", c[\"judge_mean\"], \"ticket+examples\"))\n    return found\n# Run hourly: alerts(summarise(last_hour), summarise(same_hour_last_week)).\n```","tradeoffs":["Judge sampling at 1% is cheap but a small tenant may get five scored requests a day; stratified sampling fixes coverage at the cost of a more complex sampler.","Proxy metrics are free and fast but indirect; a refusal-rate drop can mean a better model or a jailbroken one, so proxies trigger investigation, not conclusions.","Fine slicing catches per-tenant regressions but multiplies alert series and shrinks samples per slice; past a point the right move is anomaly detection on the slice set rather than fixed thresholds.","Online judges give continuous quality signal but inherit judge bias and drift when the judge model itself is updated; pin the judge and re-calibrate it against human labels periodically.","Capturing full prompts and outputs makes every alert debuggable but creates a sensitive data store with retention and access obligations; hashing or redaction trades debuggability for safety."],"pitfalls":["Monitoring only HTTP health; the symptom is a week of green dashboards followed by a customer escalation about answers that have been wrong since the last model update.","Alerting on raw judge scores without a sample floor; the symptom is 3 a.m. pages caused by twelve unlucky requests, followed by the alert being muted.","Events not tagged with bundle, prompt and model version; the symptom is a regression you can see but cannot attribute to a release, so rollback becomes guesswork.","Treating thumbs-down counts as representative; the symptom is optimising for the loudest segment while the silent majority's task completion falls.","Dashboards without drill-down; the symptom is an alert that says \"quality down 8%\" and a two-hour hunt for a single example.","Letting the judge model auto-update; the symptom is a step change in every quality series on the day the vendor shipped, with no change in your system."],"when":"From the first production deployment: operational metrics and the cheapest proxies (schema failures,\nrefusals, length, finish reason) sliced by version cost almost nothing and catch the loud failures.\nAdd sampled judging once you have a rubric that correlates with human labels, and add feedback\ncapture as soon as the product surface allows it. Invest in slicing when you have more than a handful\nof tenants or intents. What to measure is determined by what the system is for: a RAG assistant\nneeds groundedness and citation rate; an agent needs tool-failure and step-count distributions; a\nstructured extractor needs schema validity and field-level agreement on a sample. In interviews, the\nexpected answer distinguishes proxies from quality, explains label latency, and shows how an alert\nleads to examples and then to a new eval case.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Emits version-tagged events, implements the proxy checks and the sampler, and builds the drill-down from an alert to the traces that caused it.","architect":"Chooses the signal set per use case, the judge sampling and calibration policy, the slicing dimensions, and which alerts page versus ticket, within the data-retention constraints.","fde":"Agrees with the customer which outcome metric counts as success, wires their feedback channels into the monitor, and reviews the per-tenant slice with them during hypercare."},"check":{"q":"Your support assistant's provider model alias updated overnight. Nothing crashed. Describe how a\nwell-designed monitor would surface the change within hours and what it would show you.","a":"Operational series would show a cost-per-request or latency step change tagged to the same bundle\nid, since the bundle did not change but the model behind the alias did. Behavioural proxies would move\nfirst: output length distribution, refusal rate, tool-call rate or schema-failure rate shifting at the\nsame timestamp across all tenants, which is the signature of an upstream change rather than a user\nmix change. The sampled online judge would show a score shift within a few hours once the minimum\nsample accrues, with the lowest-scoring traces attached for review. Explicit feedback would lag by a\nday. The monitor would also expose that the model id reported in responses differs from the pinned\nsnapshot, which is the root cause; the fix is to pin snapshots and treat alias updates as releases\nthat go through shadow or canary."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are behavioural proxies such as refusal rate and schema-failure rate monitored on every request while judge scores are sampled?","options":["Judge scores are only valid on requests with user feedback","Proxies are deterministic and nearly free to compute, whereas a judge call costs about as much as the request it scores","Proxies are more accurate measures of answer quality than judges","Sampling is required by data-protection rules for judged content"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Proxies are cheap functions of the output, so full coverage is affordable; judging duplicates inference cost, so it is sampled and used to decide where expensive human labels go."},{"q":"A monitor reports overall judge score unchanged after a release, yet one enterprise customer complains loudly. What design gap most likely explains it?","options":["The judge model was not run at temperature zero","The release did not include a prompt change","Metrics were not sliced by tenant, so a large drop in one tenant was hidden by the aggregate","Explicit feedback was not collected from that customer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aggregates average across tenants and intents. A regression confined to one segment can be invisible in the overall mean, which is why every series is sliced by version, tenant and intent."},{"q":"Which alert design most reduces false pages from a noisy quality metric?","options":["Alert on the metric's raw value every five minutes","Alert only when a human reviewer confirms the drop","Require a minimum sample per window and compare against a time-matched baseline before alerting","Replace the judge with a keyword match"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Judged metrics vary widely on small samples and with time of day. A sample floor and a time-matched baseline separate real shifts from noise and from weekly seasonality."}]}]});
